1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,576
Every outdated app on your PC carries known
security vulnerabilities.

2
00:00:04,726 --> 00:00:08,031
Across a whole machine, that can easily add up
to hundreds.

3
00:00:09,181 --> 00:00:15,484
Chrome, AnyDesk, OneDrive, Adobe Reader. Every
app stuck on an old version carries
vulnerabilities

4
00:00:15,484 --> 00:00:17,799
the whole world already knows about.

5
00:00:17,949 --> 00:00:20,808
Tracking all of them by hand? Nobody wants that.

6
00:00:21,858 --> 00:00:27,259
Meet mr-ducksafe: a free, open-source
vulnerability scanner for the software installed

7
00:00:27,259 --> 00:00:27,949
on Windows.

8
00:00:28,099 --> 00:00:31,173
One single .exe. No install, no account.

9
00:00:32,423 --> 00:00:35,987
First, it reads your installed programs from the
Windows Registry.

10
00:00:36,137 --> 00:00:40,428
Then it maps each one to an official product
identifier, called a CPE.

11
00:00:40,578 --> 00:00:45,758
Next, it queries the National Vulnerability
Database for issues that affect the exact

12
00:00:45,758 --> 00:00:46,795
version you have.

13
00:00:46,945 --> 00:00:51,078
Finally, it explains the risk in plain language,
and hands you the fix.

14
00:00:52,328 --> 00:00:53,944
All it takes is one command.

15
00:00:55,328 --> 00:00:58,760
A hundred and forty-three programs scanned.
Twenty-one matched.

16
00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:03,587
On this machine, that meant five hundred and
twenty-five known vulnerabilities,

17
00:01:03,587 --> 00:01:07,375
boiled down to just fourteen clear actions, most
critical first.

18
00:01:08,525 --> 00:01:11,049
The raw database entry is technical and long.

19
00:01:11,199 --> 00:01:16,205
mr-ducksafe tells you what it actually means:
the application can be hijacked through

20
00:01:16,205 --> 00:01:17,088
a memory error.

21
00:01:17,238 --> 00:01:20,040
And it gives you the fix: a single winget
command.

22
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:26,515
Want every CVE for a single app? Filter by name,
and switch on the detailed table.

23
00:01:28,854 --> 00:01:30,450
It is built for CI, too.

24
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:36,068
If findings cross your fail-on threshold, it
exits with code one, and the pipeline turns

25
00:01:36,068 --> 00:01:36,317
red.

26
00:01:36,467 --> 00:01:38,728
The full report is saved as JSON.

27
00:01:39,978 --> 00:01:43,109
Every scan is stored in a local database on your
machine.

28
00:01:43,259 --> 00:01:48,656
Scanning is fully local. Only vendor, product
and version ever leave your computer.

29
00:01:48,656 --> 00:01:49,631
Zero telemetry.

30
00:01:50,881 --> 00:01:54,673
Grab mr-ducksafe on GitHub, and start scanning
with one command.

31
00:01:54,823 --> 00:01:57,399
If you find it useful, give the project a star.

